 3. `inject-contract.mjs` injects `CDP6-CONTRACT v1` at SubagentStart (as `cdp5-agents/hooks/inject-contract.mjs:21-25`) and binds the one unbound pending spawn of that `agent_type` to the `agent_id` from the hook input (hooks.md:2541 input; KR-10); because step 2 allows at most one unbound pending spawn per role, the binding does not rest on start order (A-18 records the order for concurrent spawns of different roles). For teammates see §3.4 item 2.
 4. At `SubagentHandback` (`check-handback.mjs`, as `cdp5-agents/hooks/check-handback.mjs:46-72` reads `tool_input.message` and `agent_id`): acknowledgement, the `run:`/`task:` line, whose task must equal the bound spawn's task (a mismatch denies), hedges, written-file claims (each `wrote: <path>` line names a file whose mtime is later than the spawn time), PII lines; first failure denies once with the reason. When the contract cannot be verified the handback is denied, not passed (CDP5's branch at `check-handback.mjs:54-61` passes it; CDP6 does not copy that). Refusals at capture time (a busy lock, an unreadable run record or `counters.json`, a malformed `index.jsonl`, a capture file of the same name) also deny once per agent id; a later handback of that agent id that still fails is neither denied nor captured and leaves a `hooks observed` line with decision `none`, so the phase exit finds no capture and fails closed (UC-01 Exit). Only an unverifiable contract and a missing `agent_id` deny every time, also without a data directory; the first ends in STOP-8. A counter-role handback whose capture form (§5.3) fails `verdict-check.mjs` is denied once with the reasons, so the role can restate, also after an earlier deny of the same agent id for another cause, since the marker keeps the capture-form deny apart (SL-2 fix audit F-D); a second failing handback is captured with `validator: rejected`, which the gate does not count as a pass; one that has a verdict (b) or (c) blocks until the owner's record (§5.3 gate decision table) (SL-2 outside review gap 5). A handback whose bound spawn is already closed with a capture of the same message sha256 (the handback call was denied by another hook after the capture) gets no decision and no second capture. Without an absolute `CLAUDE_PLUGIN_DATA` no run is active and nothing is captured, so `check-handback.mjs` gives no decision, as the UC-10 error row says for every hook (SL-2 T2 review, ledger 0021). This route captures only in permission mode `auto`: Claude Code provides `SubagentHandback` "only in auto mode" (tools-reference.md:53, fetched 2026-11-05), runs it without a prompt even if the classifier objects, and on a hook `deny` tells the subagent up to three times that its report was not delivered (hooks.md:2012-2014, fetched 2026-11-05). In `default` (the CLI's "manual mode") a worker has no handback tool and ends with its report as text (`SubagentStop` `last_assistant_message`, hooks.md:2608, fetched 2026-11-05), which no stage-1 hook reads: in live P1 run 1 both devils-advocate agents had Read, Grep and Glob only (record `live-p1-run-1.md:30`). The stage-1 live run therefore runs in `auto` (§0 item 34, §11.3 item 3), probe A-23 shows the tool and its input fields per mode (§11.1), and until `check-stop.mjs` (SL-2b) a finished agent without a capture stops the run by step 7.
 5. The accepted message is written to `S/verdicts/<task>-<role>-<agent-id>.md` (`<role>` is the role name of the handback's `agent_type`; the file name without `.md` is the `capture id`, recorded as `id` on the index line) with sha256, the bound spawn's `target:` hashes and an index line (`captured verdict`); `verdict-check.mjs` validates role outputs (O16): mapping line, `fresh context: yes`, `rejected alternative provided: yes` for devils-advocate (a pass with `no` is invalid, orchestrate SKILL.md:68), `conflict of interest:` line (C13; a line that names a stake records the capture `conflicted`, STOP-5, whatever its verdict; `verdict-check.mjs` marks it `cleared` only on a later capture whose bound `target:` lines include the conflicted capture's `target:` hash, whose `agent_type` differs from the conflicted role, whose own `conflict of interest:` line reads `none`, whose bound spawn's brief header carried `clears: <capture id of the conflicted capture>` (§5.3), and whose output has one `disposition:` line per conflicted item, that is per objection `F-Pk-n` and per premise of the conflicted capture (premises cited as `<capture id>:premise-<n>`), or on `run.mjs object` records per item, which the gate counts through `lib/verdict-index.mjs` whatever the conflicted capture's validator result and also when the capture is `stale`; the items of a rejected conflicted capture are read from its file, and one that lists no item is cleared by the record `<capture id>:conflict` (SL-2 fix review F-1 and F-2, audit F-C and NV-1; owner decision §0 item 19 (2), r5-TM T5-3, r5-DA X-2); the clearing capture's own verdict counts at the gate like any capture; the clearing branch is built in SL-5, and until then only the `run.mjs object` records clear), premises section (O4), every `block` objection traceable; a devils-advocate verdict "not decidable without <evidence>" is recorded as no pass, so `decision-check.mjs`, which accepts (a), (a) with conditions and a (b) or (c) cleared by